const replaceMission = (code, i, title, source, task, answer) => { workshops[code].missions[i]=m(title,source,task,answer); };
replaceMission('1.2',2,'Three equipment stations','Mini atlas index: Brazil—South America; China—East Asia; Ireland—west of Great Britain. Map line A is labelled7 cm. Classroom observation: choose a visible feature.','Station1: locate Brazil using the supplied index. Station2: draw a7 cm line; if no ruler is available, interpret the labelled line instead. Station3: record an observation without guessing a cause.','Brazil is in South America; a ruler reading should start at zero and include cm; a visible observation needs no invented reason. The displayed index replaces an atlas for this task.');
replaceMission('1.4',0,'A crossing changes a place','Iron Bridge: River Severn; completed1779, opened1781. Separate fictional valley: before, a ferry connects two banks; after, a bridge carries people and goods.','Draw a before/after crossing diagram. Mark the verified facts separately from possible effects in the fictional valley.','The dates and river refer to Iron Bridge. Faster or more reliable crossing is a possibility in the fictional valley, not a measured historical result.');
replaceMission('1.4',1,'Then-and-now investigation','Fictional valley: before, ferry and two farms; after, bridge, workshop and three homes. River and hill remain.','Make a stayed/changed table with four observations. Suggest how the crossing might support the workshop.','River/hill stay; crossing method/buildings change. Better access may help goods and workers; the diagram cannot establish the cause.');
replaceMission('1.4',2,'Compare with a local change','Fictional local case: a footbridge replaces a long detour; a stall opens near the crossing. Earlier fictional valley: bridge and workshop replace a ferry crossing.','Compare the two cases using cause → possible effect. Name evidence to test each explanation.','Access may help stalls and workshops reach customers; opening dates and user counts could test the link. These are imagined cases, not claims about Bishkek.');
replaceMission('3.1',0,'Deliver four postcards','Map facts: England, Scotland and Wales are on Great Britain; Northern Ireland is on the island of Ireland. North Sea east of Great Britain; Irish Sea between the islands.','Locate four UK countries on the screen map. Write three relative-location clues for a partner.','Wales is west of England; Scotland is north of England; Northern Ireland is across the Irish Sea.');
replaceMission('3.2',0,'Build two map keys','England, Scotland, Wales, Northern Ireland: political divisions. Scottish Highlands: uplands in northern Great Britain. Thames: a river in southern England.','Create political and physical keys. Sketch where the named examples belong using the screen map and source.','Borders are political; highlands and rivers are physical. A political boundary does not necessarily follow a ridge.');
replaceMission('3.2',1,'The boundary detective','Wales lies west of England. The Scottish Highlands are in northern Great Britain. These are a country and a physical region, respectively.','Explain why a country outline cannot simply be labelled a mountain range. Add both types of information to a schematic key.','A country is a political division; a mountain region describes relief. Different layers answer different questions.');
replaceMission('4.7',0,'A valley visitor plan','Langdale is a Lake District glacial valley. Classroom path counts: A80 visitors/hour, B20. A broad floor and steep sides are features of a glacial trough.','Explain the valley shape, compare the counts and propose a management investigation.','Ice erosion widens and deepens valleys. A has four times B’s visitors in that hour; repeat counts and inspect path condition.');
